# 10. ASSESSMENT MODES

P37 must present a capability-aware assessment workflow.

## Mode A — Geometric Ventilation Potential

This mode examines room geometry, operable opening information, opening areas, room connectivity, and potential intake/outlet paths.

Possible outputs:

- Openable area, where dimensions support it.
- Opening-area-to-floor-area ratio as a geometric descriptor.
- Availability of plausible intake and outlet routes.
- Potential for cross-ventilation.
- Potential for single-sided ventilation.
- Potential for high/low opening pathways.
- Missing input details.
- Physical or layout constraints.
- Potential conflicts with other design requirements.

A geometric ratio is not itself proof of sufficient ventilation.

Do not claim compliance with a building-code opening-area requirement unless a valid jurisdiction-specific criterion is selected, correctly applied, and fully supported by the relevant data.

## Mode B — Qualitative Airflow-Path Screening

Use supported geometry and explicit assumptions to explain likely airflow opportunities and limitations.

This mode can identify a potential opening-to-opening route, an interrupted transfer path, or a room with limited supported openings.

It must not output a fabricated airflow rate or air-change rate.

## Mode C — Quantified Preliminary Airflow Estimate

Offer this mode only if the required physical inputs and supported calculation method are available.

At a minimum, assess whether the method has adequate information about:

- Opening dimensions and effective free area.
- Opening configuration and discharge behaviour.
- Room geometry where volume or distribution matters.
- Pressure-driving conditions.
- Relevant wind or stack boundary conditions.
- Complete intake-to-outlet paths.
- Necessary assumptions about air properties and losses.
- The validity of the selected airflow model.

If one or more essential inputs are missing, do not silently populate them with arbitrary defaults. Return a reasoned incomplete-data result, or allow a clearly labelled lower-confidence screening mode.

## Mode D — Scenario Comparison

Allow a user to compare reasonable opening and environmental scenarios.

Examples may include:

- Closed versus partially open windows.
- Single-sided versus cross-ventilation layout.
- Alternative intake and outlet positions.
- Internal transfer opening closed versus open.
- Lower versus higher wind conditions.
- Different indoor/outdoor temperature differences.
- User-selected operating schedules.
- A hypothetical ventilation design before applying changes.

Every scenario must preserve the same project identity, clearly expose relevant input differences, and state which outputs are genuinely comparable.

A change in opening configuration must not be confused with a change in weather or room geometry.

## Mode E — Insufficient-Data Guidance

When meaningful quantification is not possible, explain:

- Which required inputs are missing.
- Why those inputs matter.
- Whether geometric or qualitative assessment remains available.
- What the user could provide to enable a stronger analysis.
- Which conclusions cannot currently be drawn.

Do not simply display “calculation failed” if the feature can explain the underlying limitation.

# 11. WIND-DRIVEN AND STACK-DRIVEN CALCULATIONS

Use a documented, physically coherent calculation strategy. Do not mix incompatible simplified formulas or add individually estimated airflow magnitudes in a way that ignores direction and pressure relationships.

## 11.1 Wind-driven pressure

Where appropriate and supported, a preliminary wind-pressure relation may use the recognised physical form:

ΔP_wind = ½ × ρ × v² × ΔCp

Where:

- ΔP_wind is the pressure difference in pascals.
- ρ is air density in kg/m³.
- v is the applicable wind speed in m/s.
- ΔCp is the difference between the relevant pressure coefficients at the connected openings.

This relationship is a model input, not a universal guarantee of the pressure difference across a building.

The correct wind direction, opening orientation, reference height, building exposure, and applicable pressure coefficients matter.

Do not assign fabricated pressure coefficients to a particular building. If they cannot be determined from the available validated model or source data, keep the analysis qualitative or explicitly scenario-based.

## 11.2 Buoyancy or stack-driven pressure

Temperature differences and vertical separation can create buoyancy-driven pressure differences.

If a simplified stack-pressure method is implemented, document:

- The exact equation.
- The temperature and pressure assumptions.
- Air-density treatment.
- Relevant opening elevations.
- The height and vertical arrangement of the connected openings.
- The conditions under which the approximation is valid.
- Its limitations for real multi-storey or multi-opening flow.

Do not claim that a single room-height value automatically determines real building airflow.

## 11.3 Opening flow equation

Where the selected physical model justifies it, the opening-flow relation may use a documented orifice-style form:

Q = Cd × A_eff × √(2 × |ΔP| / ρ)

Where:

- Q is the flow magnitude in m³/s.
- Cd is the discharge coefficient appropriate to the model and opening.
- A_eff is the effective area in m².
- ΔP is the pressure difference in pascals.
- ρ is air density in kg/m³.

This form applies only under the assumptions of the selected opening model.

For multiple openings, series restrictions, parallel paths, bidirectional openings, or multi-zone systems, implement an appropriate validated pressure-flow/network method. Do not use a single-opening equation indiscriminately for an entire building.

If the model cannot account for pressure interactions or relevant losses adequately, downgrade the output to a qualified screening result.

Do not add wind-driven and stack-driven flow magnitudes blindly. Their effects depend on direction, pressure distribution, opening geometry, and the complete airflow network.

## 11.4 Air changes per hour

Where a defensible volumetric airflow rate and room volume are available, calculate:

ACH = Q × 3600 / V

Where:

- ACH is air changes per hour.
- Q is volumetric flow in m³/s.
- V is room air volume in m³.
- 3600 converts seconds to hours.

Check units, signs, volume validity, flow direction, and the method's applicability.

Make it clear that air changes per hour derived from an estimate are not equivalent to measured air changes or proof of sufficient outdoor-air ventilation.

Do not display ACH when room volume or airflow has not been established appropriately.

## 11.5 Numerical handling

All calculation functions must:

- Reject non-finite or invalid physical inputs.
- Prevent division by zero.
- Avoid invalid square-root operations.
- Validate effective opening areas and discharge coefficients.
- Use explicit unit conversions.
- Preserve internal precision.
- Apply documented display rounding.
- Produce deterministic results for equivalent inputs and model versions.
- Return structured warnings instead of silent fallback values.
- Identify inputs that materially change the result.

Include independently calculated test fixtures for every supported quantitative method.

If the implementation cannot implement and validate a defensible airflow model within the current architecture, ship a strong geometry/qualitative screening workflow and explicitly mark quantified flow estimation unsupported for that scenario.

# 12. INDOOR AIR QUALITY, MOISTURE, AND THERMAL CONSIDERATIONS

Ventilation is not an unconditional benefit regardless of outdoor conditions.

Where the data supports it, help users recognise trade-offs involving outdoor air quality, humidity, heat, moisture, and comfort.

## 12.1 Outdoor air quality

Allow the user to provide or import appropriately sourced outdoor air-quality observations where supported.

Potential considerations include particulate matter, smoke, dust, ozone or other relevant contaminants, but do not invent measured concentrations.

Do not automatically recommend opening windows when the supplied outdoor evidence identifies a material contamination concern.

Do not assume outdoor air is clean.

Do not calculate actual indoor pollutant concentrations from airflow alone unless the required contaminant source rates, initial conditions, mixing assumptions, removal mechanisms, and valid calculation model are implemented and documented.

Do not introduce a live air-quality integration unless an authorised existing service supports it or it is explicitly within the permitted implementation environment.

## 12.2 Temperature and humidity

Where relevant input data exists, identify when the outdoor/indoor conditions may make ventilation thermally or moisture-wise undesirable.

Distinguish:

- Potential airflow.
- Outdoor-air temperature.
- Indoor temperature.
- Humidity-related inputs.
- Preliminary comfort implications.
- Actual validated comfort calculation.

Do not claim that natural ventilation will cool or dehumidify a room merely because an opening is available.

Do not run a full energy or thermal-dynamics simulation under P37.

Do not replace P35's climate-material assessment or claim moisture compatibility based only on airflow.

## 12.3 Rain, insects, security, and privacy

Where relevant data exists, provide prompts or constraint warnings about:

- Rain exposure through an opening.
- Screens or other reductions in effective opening area.
- Window restrictors or limited opening angles.
- Security or privacy restrictions reported by the user.
- The availability of a suitable safe opening state.

Do not infer that a window can safely be opened to its full extent from its nominal dimensions.

Do not make legal or security-safety claims without relevant evidence.

# 13. ACOUSTIC, ACCESSIBILITY, AND ARCHITECTURAL TRADE-OFFS

P37 must support integrated design reasoning, not optimise airflow in isolation.

## 13.1 P36 — Acoustic Comfort Planner

Use the actual P36 acoustic contracts to identify relevant concerns when appropriate.

Examples include:

- An open window may provide a potential external-air path while admitting additional outdoor noise.
- A scenario may conflict with a confirmed acoustic privacy requirement.
- An internal door may form part of a ventilation path but also affect the acoustic separation between rooms.
- An acoustic assessment based on different opening assumptions may become stale after a scenario change.

Do not claim a predictable increase in dB without supported measurement or calculation.

Do not modify P36's acoustic data.

Keep a ventilation opportunity separate from its acoustic trade-offs.

## 13.2 P34 — Universal Accessibility Review

Any proposed layout or opening modification must preserve the relevant P34 constraints.

Consider, where applicable:

- Clear access routes.
- Door clearances.
- Accessible circulation.
- Turning space.
- Thresholds.
- Potential obstructions caused by a proposed vent, door position, equipment, or control.

Do not narrow circulation paths or invalidate accessibility constraints just to improve a ventilation screening score.

Do not duplicate P34's accessibility engine.

A successful ventilation assessment is not proof that a design is accessible.

## 13.3 P33 — Furniture and Circulation

Use actual furniture and geometry data only when relevant.

If a furniture object may obstruct a supported airflow path, explain the geometric reason and the limitations. Do not invent an airflow reduction coefficient based solely on furniture appearance.

Do not claim that a furniture arrangement has improved the measured or predicted airflow without a supported method.

## 13.4 P35 — Climate and materials

Reuse P35's explicit climate context and provenance where relevant.

Do not promote an unconfigured climate context into measured weather.

If a proposed opening or ventilation strategy has possible moisture or material-durability implications, surface appropriate P35 warnings without reimplementing its climate/material engine.

Preserve P35's material source data and existing calculations.

# 14. VENTILATION REQUIREMENTS AND REGULATORY CRITERIA

Do not hard-code one universal minimum opening ratio, air-change rate, or ventilation rate for every room and building type.

Natural-ventilation requirements can depend on jurisdiction, occupancy, building type, environmental conditions, opening design, outdoor-air quality, mechanical systems, and the applicable standard.

Support configurable project criteria where appropriate.

A criterion should identify relevant information such as:

- Project jurisdiction.
- Building and occupancy type.
- Applicable standard or regulation.
- Edition or adoption status.
- Required metric.
- Target or range.
- Unit.
- Applicability conditions.
- Verification or source state.
- Whether it is mandatory, project-defined, or advisory.
- Relevant date or version.

If the project has no configured applicable criterion, P37 may provide a geometric or preliminary reference assessment but must not claim regulatory compliance.

A window-area ratio must never be automatically treated as evidence that ventilation-rate or indoor-air-quality requirements have been satisfied.

Do not claim compliance with ASHRAE Standard 62.1, Standard 62.2, or a local building code merely because an estimated ACH is above a displayed number.

Inspect current public metadata for relevant source titles, versions, and scope, but do not reproduce or upload proprietary standards content into an AI tool without proper authorisation.

Do not fabricate standards clauses, equations, mandatory thresholds, editions, or regional applicability.

